{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01SPGdMLbEj22tCZodQX8utS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to generate detailed predicted data for each \"planned\" experiment in the experiment_plan, based on the \"median\" tier definition provided. Let me identify which experiments have status \"planned\":\n\n1. **exp-015** (sft_d100_qc_only) - planned\n2. **exp-007** (scale_bridging_7b) - planned\n3. **exp-014** (multi_family_validation) - planned\n4. **exp-009** (rl_observational_case_study) - planned\n5. **exp-012** (observational_72b) - planned\n\n\n6. **exp-013** (closed_weight_2signal_mode) - planned\n\nNow I'm understanding what the median tier represents: it's characterized by robust results for Qwen with directionally consistent but underpowered cross-family findings, where the main advantage of calibration over linear regression is interpretability rather than improved error metrics. The key assumptions are that Llama-3.2-3B will show the same directional effect on ARC and GSM8K but with confidence intervals that cross zero due to small sample size, MMLU domain patterns hold for Qwen while Llama data alone can't confirm the STEM gradient, and coverage remains in the 45-60% range at 95% nominal confidence even with larger N.\n\nLooking at the specific findings: the local independence violation is structural, the IRT advantage over linear regression isn't statistically significant with bootstrap p-values between 0.1-0.4, GSM8K shows persistent overcorrection that the gating mechanism properly blocks, and non-STEM domains remain misspecified which I'm acknowledging as a scope limitation. The key metrics I'm tracking show arc_gamma ranging from -2.0 to -0.5 with confidence intervals consistently excluding zero, arc calibration MAE between 0.03-0.05, raw MAE between 0.045-0.065, while GSM8K gamma stays between -0.5 and 0.5 with confidence intervals that don't exclude zero.\n\nNow I'm examining the performance across the remaining domains: MMLU STEM math shows positive gamma values around 0.5-1.1, STEM science ranges 0.3-0.9, and CS/engineering spans -0.3 to 0.5, with coverage at 95% confidence reaching 0.45-0.6 at n=24. The ranking correlation (Kendall tau) sits between 0.65-0.8, signal detection AUC for the first signal is very strong at 0.95-0.99, and the reversal effect shows a substantial Cohen's d between -2.8 and -1.2. Cross-family results indicate consistently negative arc gamma with mixed confidence interval behavior, and I'm tracking how many benchmarks pass the calibration gating criteria.\n\nLooking at the existing data to ground my predictions: ARC consistently shows forgetting effects with gamma around -1.46, GSM8K hovers near zero at 0.187, MMLU domains vary from 0.197 to 0.969 depending on subject, and the first signal achieves an AUC of 0.979 with the second signal showing a reversal of about -1 Cohen's d.\n\nFor the QC-only experiment, I expect the cleaned paraphrases to produce a slightly sharper contamination signal, but the core structural effects—position bias at 0.5B and forgetting on ARC—should remain fundamentally unchanged. The 7B scaling experiment should show contamination effects nearly disappearing on multiple-choice benchmarks, consistent with the pattern where effects are catastrophic at 0.5B, mild at 3B, and should approach negligible by 7B.\n\nWith the multi-family validation using Llama-3.2-3B across dosage levels, I'd expect the same directional gamma patterns but with confidence intervals that include zero due to the smaller sample size. The observational RL case study should show wider confidence intervals overall while maintaining directional consistency, with near-zero gamma on clean benchmarks confirming specificity. At 72B scale, contamination effects should be minimal across standard benchmarks. The degraded 2-signal mode should show significant accuracy loss compared to the 4-signal baseline.\n\nNow I'm formatting this as YAML with detailed rationales...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":41798,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":41798},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7bfc8c63-698c-463c-aad9-5de9bc2cd8ab","uuid":"6522dd49-ea2c-4e5c-800f-dcb9172bc4cb"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01SPGdMLbEj22tCZodQX8utS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntier: median\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  ContamCal characterization framework reveals structured contamination heterogeneity\n  within Qwen2.5 family: domain-level Simpson's paradox in MMLU, directional\n  benchmark-dependence (ARC forgetting vs GSM8K near-immunity), scale modulation.\n  Cross-family analysis directionally consistent but underpowered for independent\n  significance — frame as \"preliminary cross-family evidence\" not \"confirmed generality.\"\n  Calibration Claim demoted further: IRT's advantage is interpretable parameters and\n  uncertainty quantification, NOT better point estimates vs simple linear regression.\n  Coverage gap reported transparently with SBC diagnostic attributing it to local\n  independence violation at small N. Signal reversal and identifiability analysis\n  remain at full strength. Adjust Claim 1 to \"within-family characterization with\n  cross-family directional support.\" Claim 4 reworded: \"calibration provides\n  interpretable uncertainty-aware estimates competitive with simple regression.\"\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"sft_d100_qc_only\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-015\"\n    description: >\n      100% dosage SFT using only QC-pass paraphrases (7547/16697 items) on Qwen2.5-0.5B.\n      Isolates noisy vs clean contamination confound. Compare against exp-001 d100 (all paraphrases).\n    expected_results:\n      training_loss: 1.58\n      token_accuracy: 0.672\n      training_time_min: 55\n      eval_mmlu_accuracy: 0.248\n      eval_gsm8k_accuracy: 0.361\n      eval_arc_accuracy: 0.253\n      eval_humaneval_pass1: 0.073\n      mmlu_delta_vs_d000: -0.157\n      arc_delta_vs_d000: -0.144\n      gsm8k_delta_vs_d000: -0.003\n      mmlu_delta_vs_d100_all: +0.015\n      arc_delta_vs_d100_all: -0.041\n      signal_s1_auc_qc_only: 0.985\n      signal_s1_auc_d100_all: 0.501\n      irt_gamma_arc_qc_only: -1.8\n      irt_gamma_arc_d100_all: -1.2\n      gamma_difference_significant: false\n      bootstrap_p_gamma_diff: 0.22\n    result_rationale: >\n      QC-only removes noisy paraphrases (low semantic similarity, high lexical overlap) that\n      dilute contamination signal. At 0.5B, the dominant effect is still position bias\n      amplification (D045) and capacity displacement, so benchmark accuracy differences between\n      QC-only and all-paraphrases are modest (~1-4pp). The key difference is in Stage 1 signal\n      quality: S1 embedding AUC rises to ~0.985 for QC-only because the paraphrases genuinely\n      preserve semantic content (cosine >0.85), whereas d100-all includes low-quality items\n      that act as noise. However, IRT γ estimates don't differ significantly between QC-only\n      and all-paraphrase conditions (bootstrap p=0.22) because at N=1 model the comparison\n      is underpowered. The experiment confirms that paraphrase quality matters for signal\n      reliability but doesn't fundamentally change the characterization conclusions — the\n      forgetting effect on ARC and near-immunity on GSM8K are structural properties of the\n      benchmark × scale interaction, not artifacts of noisy contamination injection.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"QC-Only vs All-Paraphrase Comparison (0.5B, d100)\"\n        columns: [benchmark, d100_all_acc, d100_qc_only_acc, delta_pp, d000_baseline]\n        rows:\n          - [MMLU, 0.233, 0.248, +1.5, 0.405]\n          - [GSM8K, 0.365, 0.361, -0.4, 0.364]\n          - [ARC, 0.294, 0.253, -4.1, 0.397]\n          - [HumanEval, 0.085, 0.073, -1.2, 0.110]\n      - name: \"Signal Quality Comparison\"\n        columns: [signal, d100_all_auc, d100_qc_only_auc, interpretation]\n        rows:\n          - [S1_embedding, 0.501, 0.985, \"QC filtering restores S1 discriminability at d100\"]\n          - [S2_minkpp, 0.48, 0.51, \"No meaningful improvement — S2 fails structurally at 0.5B\"]\n          - [S4_selfcritique, 0.49, 0.52, \"Marginal improvement insufficient for practical use\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Scatter plot: per-item S1 score (QC-only training) vs semantic similarity of paraphrase. Shows threshold effect around cosine=0.85 where S1 transitions from noise to signal.\"\n    analysis: >\n      This experiment resolves Concern #22 partially: QC filtering improves signal quality\n      but does not change the fundamental characterization. The 0.5B forgetting on ARC persists\n      with QC-only data (-14.4pp vs -10.3pp for all-paraphrases), confirming it reflects\n      genuine capacity displacement + contamination interaction, not merely noise from bad\n      paraphrases. Paper treatment: report as robustness check showing results are not\n      confounded by paraphrase quality. Recommend QC filtering as best practice for future\n      controlled contamination studies.\n\n  - name: \"scale_bridging_7b\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-007\"\n    description: >\n      Qwen2.5-7B controlled SFT injection at 5 dosage levels (0%, 5%, 25%, 50%, 100%).\n      Validates scale modulation hypothesis: effects negligible at 7B for MC benchmarks.\n    expected_results:\n      sft_training_hours_per_run: 7.5\n      total_gpu_hours: 37.5\n      d000_mmlu: 0.718\n      d000_gsm8k: 0.802\n      d000_arc: 0.893\n      d000_humaneval: 0.354\n      d005_mmlu: 0.719\n      d005_gsm8k: 0.803\n      d005_arc: 0.891\n      d005_humaneval: 0.366\n      d025_mmlu: 0.720\n      d025_gsm8k: 0.807\n      d025_arc: 0.889\n      d025_humaneval: 0.378\n      d100_mmlu: 0.723\n      d100_gsm8k: 0.813\n      d100_arc: 0.884\n      d100_humaneval: 0.415\n      mmlu_max_delta_pp: +0.5\n      gsm8k_max_delta_pp: +1.1\n      arc_max_delta_pp: -0.9\n      humaneval_max_delta_pp: +6.1\n      irt_gamma_arc_7b: -0.35\n      irt_gamma_arc_7b_ci: [-1.2, 0.5]\n      irt_gamma_arc_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      irt_gamma_gsm8k_7b: 0.12\n      irt_gamma_gsm8k_7b_ci: [-0.8, 1.0]\n      irt_gamma_mmlu_7b: 0.08\n      irt_gamma_mmlu_7b_ci: [-0.9, 1.1]\n      s1_auc_d005_arc: 0.97\n      s1_auc_d005_mmlu: 0.96\n      s2_cohens_d_7b: -0.3\n      scale_modulation_confirmed: true\n      calibration_mae_arc: 0.008\n      raw_mae_arc: 0.006\n      calibration_unnecessary: true\n    result_rationale: >\n      At 7B, the model has sufficient capacity that SFT contamination injection causes\n      negligible performance shifts on MC benchmarks (<1pp for MMLU/ARC, ~1pp for GSM8K).\n      This confirms the scale modulation hypothesis already visible in the 0.5B→1.5B→3B\n      progression: 0.5B shows catastrophic forgetting (-17pp ARC), 3B shows mild effects\n      (<2pp), 7B shows effects within noise floor. The IRT γ estimate for ARC at 7B is\n      directionally negative but CI includes zero — the forgetting effect exists but is\n      too small to detect with N=5 models at this scale. HumanEval shows the only meaningful\n      dose-response (+6pp at d100) because code generation benefits from memorized patterns\n      even at large scales. S2 (Min-K++) Cohen's d is only -0.3 at 7B — the reversal\n      effect attenuates with scale (vs -1.98 at 0.5B), but S2 still fails to provide\n      positive discrimination. Critically, calibration is unnecessary at 7B for MC\n      benchmarks: raw MAE (0.006) is already lower than calibrated MAE (0.008) because\n      the contamination effect is smaller than IRT estimation noise. This validates the\n      gating criterion — ContamCal correctly identifies when NOT to calibrate.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"7B Dosage-Accuracy Table\"\n        columns: [benchmark, d000, d005, d025, d100, max_delta_pp, direction]\n        rows:\n          - [MMLU, 0.718, 0.719, 0.720, 0.723, +0.5, \"negligible inflation\"]\n          - [GSM8K, 0.802, 0.803, 0.807, 0.813, +1.1, \"slight inflation\"]\n          - [ARC, 0.893, 0.891, 0.889, 0.884, -0.9, \"negligible forgetting\"]\n          - [HumanEval, 0.354, 0.366, 0.378, 0.415, +6.1, \"moderate inflation\"]\n      - name: \"Scale Modulation Summary (ARC forgetting, d100 vs d000)\"\n        columns: [scale, delta_pp, gamma_mean, gamma_ci_excludes_zero]\n        rows:\n          - [0.5B, -17.5, -1.46, true]\n          - [1.5B, -0.7, -0.8, false]\n          - [3B, -0.3, -0.5, false]\n          - [7B, -0.9, -0.35, false]\n      - name: \"Signal Effectiveness at 7B\"\n        columns: [signal, d005_auc, d100_cohens_d, interpretation]\n        rows:\n          - [S1_embedding, 0.97, 2.8, \"Remains effective — provenance signal scale-invariant\"]\n          - [S2_minkpp, 0.52, -0.3, \"Reversal attenuates but still non-discriminative\"]\n          - [S4_selfcritique, 0.53, 0.6, \"Marginal at all scales\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Line plot: ARC accuracy delta (d100-d000) vs model scale (0.5B, 1.5B, 3B, 7B). Shows asymptotic approach to zero, confirming scale modulation is monotonic.\"\n      - \"Heatmap: S2 Cohen's d across scales (0.5B, 1.5B, 3B, 7B) × dosages (d005, d025, d100). Shows reversal concentrated at small scales.\"\n    analysis: >\n      7B results confirm the three-regime taxonomy: (1) catastrophic capacity displacement\n      at 0.5B, (2) mild-to-moderate effects at 1.5-3B, (3) near-immunity at 7B for MC\n      benchmarks. The scale threshold for \"contamination matters\" is approximately 1-3B\n      for Qwen2.5 architecture. Paper framing: \"contamination-driven score inflation\n      requires scale-aware analysis — applying contamination corrections to large models\n      on MC benchmarks may introduce more noise than it removes.\" This supports the\n      calibration gating criterion and positions ContamCal as knowing when calibration\n      is unnecessary. The 7B HumanEval result (+6pp) is the exception — code generation\n      benefits from memorization at all scales, consistent with the benchmark-dependence\n      narrative.\n\n  - name: \"multi_family_validation\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-014\"\n    description: >\n      Controlled SFT injection on Llama-3.2-3B at 3 dosage levels (0%, 25%, 100%).\n      Tests cross-family generality of contamination characterization.\n    expected_results:\n      sft_training_hours: 12\n      d000_mmlu: 0.455\n      d000_gsm8k: 0.312\n      d000_arc: 0.685\n      d000_humaneval: 0.183\n      d025_mmlu: 0.458\n      d025_gsm8k: 0.318\n      d025_arc: 0.672\n      d025_humaneval: 0.195\n      d100_mmlu: 0.463\n      d100_gsm8k: 0.325\n      d100_arc: 0.651\n      d100_humaneval: 0.220\n      arc_delta_d100_vs_d000_pp: -3.4\n      gsm8k_delta_d100_vs_d000_pp: +1.3\n      mmlu_delta_d100_vs_d000_pp: +0.8\n      arc_gamma_llama: -0.85\n      arc_gamma_llama_ci: [-2.1, 0.4]\n      arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      gsm8k_gamma_llama: 0.22\n      gsm8k_gamma_llama_ci: [-1.3, 1.7]\n      gsm8k_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      s1_auc_d025_arc: 0.96\n      s1_auc_d025_mmlu: 0.94\n      s2_cohens_d_llama_3b: -0.8\n      cross_family_arc_gamma_sign_consistent: true\n      cross_family_gsm8k_gamma_sign_consistent: true\n      cross_family_significance: \"directional only\"\n      n_models_llama: 3\n      calibration_mae_arc_llama: 0.042\n      raw_mae_arc_llama: 0.038\n      kendall_tau_llama: 0.67\n    result_rationale: >\n      In median tier, Llama-3.2-3B shows the same directional patterns as Qwen2.5: negative\n      γ on ARC (forgetting) and near-zero γ on GSM8K (immunity). However, with only N=3\n      models (d000/d025/d100), CIs are too wide to exclude zero for any benchmark. The ARC\n      forgetting magnitude is smaller than Qwen2.5-0.5B but comparable to Qwen2.5-3B\n      (-3.4pp vs -0.3pp for Qwen-3B), suggesting Llama-3.2-3B's 3B parameters place it in\n      the \"mild effect\" regime. The directional consistency (both families show ARC forgetting,\n      GSM8K near-immunity) supports contamination effects being a benchmark × domain property\n      rather than a model-family artifact — but the evidence is underpowered for independent\n      statistical significance. S1 embedding signal transfers well cross-family (AUC=0.96)\n      because it relies on semantic similarity which is model-agnostic. S2 Min-K++ shows\n      reversal (Cohen's d = -0.8) at Llama-3B, weaker than Qwen-0.5B (-1.98) but still\n      misleading — confirming reversal is not Qwen-specific. Calibration MAE (0.042) is\n      slightly worse than raw MAE (0.038) because N=3 is far below the N≥50 identifiability\n      threshold, illustrating the paper's own recommendation against calibration at very low N.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Llama-3.2-3B Dosage-Accuracy\"\n        columns: [benchmark, d000, d025, d100, delta_pp]\n        rows:\n          - [MMLU, 0.455, 0.458, 0.463, +0.8]\n          - [GSM8K, 0.312, 0.318, 0.325, +1.3]\n          - [ARC, 0.685, 0.672, 0.651, -3.4]\n          - [HumanEval, 0.183, 0.195, 0.220, +3.7]\n      - name: \"Cross-Family γ Comparison\"\n        columns: [benchmark, qwen_3B_gamma, qwen_3B_ci_excl_zero, llama_3B_gamma, llama_3B_ci_excl_zero, sign_consistent]\n        rows:\n          - [ARC, -1.46, true, -0.85, false, yes]\n          - [GSM8K, 0.19, false, 0.22, false, yes]\n          - [MMLU, 0.52, false, 0.18, false, yes]\n      - name: \"Signal Transfer Cross-Family\"\n        columns: [signal, qwen_0.5B_auc, qwen_3B_auc, llama_3B_auc]\n        rows:\n          - [S1_embedding_d005, 0.979, 0.97, 0.96]\n          - [S2_minkpp_cohens_d, -1.98, -0.6, -0.8]\n          - [S4_selfcritique_d005, 0.481, 0.52, 0.50]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Forest plot: ARC γ estimates with 95% CIs across Qwen-0.5B, Qwen-1.5B, Qwen-3B, Llama-3.2-3B. All negative, Llama CI spans zero.\"\n      - \"Bar chart: S2 Cohen's d reversal magnitude across model families and scales.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Cross-family validation achieves directional consistency but not independent significance.\n      Paper framing: \"Preliminary cross-family evidence suggests contamination-induced\n      forgetting on ARC and near-immunity on GSM8K are benchmark properties, not model-family\n      artifacts. However, with N=3 Llama models, independent significance is not achievable —\n      we report directional consistency as supporting evidence for the Qwen-based\n      characterization, not as standalone proof of generality.\" This is the median tier's\n      core distinction from the best tier (which would show CI excluding zero for Llama ARC)\n      and the worst tier (which would show inconsistent sign direction).\n\n  - name: \"rl_observational_case_study\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-009\"\n    description: >\n      Apply detection signals to existing RL-trained models (Qwen2.5-Instruct 0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B,\n      DeepSeek-R1-Distill-1.5B/7B). Fit IRT with wider CIs. Expected regret quantification.\n    expected_results:\n      models_analyzed: 6\n      qwen_instruct_05b_mmlu: 0.246\n      qwen_instruct_15b_mmlu: 0.578\n      qwen_instruct_3b_mmlu: 0.605\n      qwen_instruct_7b_mmlu: 0.742\n      deepseek_r1_15b_mmlu: 0.585\n      deepseek_r1_7b_mmlu: 0.755\n      gamma_arc_observational: -0.45\n      gamma_arc_ci: [-1.8, 0.9]\n      gamma_arc_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      gamma_gsm8k_observational: 0.31\n      gamma_gsm8k_ci: [-0.6, 1.2]\n      gamma_gsm8k_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      gamma_mmlu_observational: 0.18\n      gamma_mmlu_ci: [-0.7, 1.1]\n      near_zero_gamma_check_clean: true\n      clean_benchmark_gamma: 0.04\n      clean_benchmark_gamma_ci: [-0.8, 0.9]\n      expected_regret_uncalibrated: 0.15\n      expected_regret_calibrated: 0.12\n      expected_regret_remove_rescore: 0.14\n      regret_reduction_pct: 20\n      coverage_95_observational: 0.50\n      kendall_tau_calibrated: 0.72\n      s1_auc_instruct_models: 0.68\n      s4_regime_differentiation: true\n      calibration_gating_pass: 0\n    result_rationale: >\n      Observational analysis of RL-trained models produces wider CIs than controlled SFT\n      because (a) no ground-truth contamination labels exist, (b) the models span different\n      training pipelines (Instruct vs R1-Distill), and (c) exposure scores from S1 are\n      noisier without controlled contamination (AUC ~0.68 vs 0.97 in controlled setting).\n      In median tier, all γ CIs include zero — the framework cannot achieve significance\n      on observational data with N=6 heterogeneous models. This is consistent with the\n      paper's own N≥50 recommendation. The near-zero γ check on a known-clean benchmark\n      (specificity test) passes, confirming the framework doesn't hallucinate contamination\n      effects where none exist. Expected regret analysis shows modest improvement (20%)\n      from calibration over raw scores for top-5 model selection, but this is not\n      statistically significant with bootstrapping. Calibration gating criterion fails for\n      all benchmarks (γ not significant) — correctly identifying that observational data\n      at this N is insufficient for reliable calibration. Paper frames as \"when exposure\n      signals are weaker and models heterogeneous, ContamCal correctly reports uncertainty\n      rather than producing overconfident corrections.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Observational Model Performance\"\n        columns: [model, mmlu, gsm8k, arc, humaneval]\n        rows:\n          - [Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct, 0.246, 0.388, 0.282, 0.0]\n          - [Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct, 0.578, 0.593, 0.765, 0.250]\n          - [Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct, 0.605, 0.760, 0.830, 0.500]\n          - [Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, 0.742, 0.850, 0.905, 0.610]\n          - [DeepSeek-R1-Distill-1.5B, 0.585, 0.680, 0.720, 0.280]\n          - [DeepSeek-R1-Distill-7B, 0.755, 0.870, 0.890, 0.650]\n      - name: \"Observational IRT γ Estimates\"\n        columns: [benchmark, gamma, ci_lower, ci_upper, ci_excl_zero, gating_pass]\n        rows:\n          - [ARC, -0.45, -1.8, 0.9, false, false]\n          - [GSM8K, 0.31, -0.6, 1.2, false, false]\n          - [MMLU, 0.18, -0.7, 1.1, false, false]\n          - [Clean control, 0.04, -0.8, 0.9, false, \"N/A (specificity)\"]\n      - name: \"Expected Regret (Top-5 Selection)\"\n        columns: [method, expected_regret, improvement_vs_raw, significant]\n        rows:\n          - [Raw (uncalibrated), 0.15, \"—\", \"—\"]\n          - [ContamCal calibrated, 0.12, \"20%\", false]\n          - [Remove-and-rescore, 0.14, \"7%\", false]\n          - [Linear regression, 0.13, \"13%\", false]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Calibrated vs raw model rankings with uncertainty ribbons for top-5 selection. Shows overlapping CIs indicating low discriminative power.\"\n      - \"S1 exposure score distribution for Instruct vs SFT-clean models — shifted but overlapping, explaining lower AUC.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The observational case study honestly demonstrates ContamCal's limitations on\n      heterogeneous, non-controlled data. With N=6 models from different training pipelines\n      and noisier exposure signals (AUC=0.68), the framework correctly reports wide\n      uncertainty rather than producing spurious corrections. The specificity check\n      (near-zero γ on clean benchmark) validates that the model doesn't false-positive.\n      The S4 self-critique signal shows differentiation between Instruct and base-SFT\n      models (consistent with exp_008_instruct_rl_proxy findings), providing directional\n      evidence that RL post-training alters signal profiles. Paper framing: \"Observational\n      analysis demonstrates calibration is directionally plausible but cannot achieve\n      significance at practical N. The framework's transparency about its own limitations\n      (wide CIs, gating criterion failure) is a feature, not a bug — it prevents\n      overconfident contamination corrections in the absence of controlled ground truth.\"\n\n  - name: \"observational_72b\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-012\"\n    description: >\n      Qwen2.5-72B inference-only observational analysis. Extends ecological validity\n      to frontier model scale.\n    expected_results:\n      inference_gpu_hours: 12\n      mmlu_accuracy: 0.828\n      gsm8k_accuracy: 0.905\n      arc_accuracy: 0.952\n      humaneval_pass1: 0.720\n      s1_exposure_mean: 0.42\n      s1_exposure_std: 0.18\n      s2_minkpp_mean: 1.71\n      s4_selfcritique_mean: -0.88\n      gamma_arc_72b: -0.15\n      gamma_arc_72b_ci: [-1.5, 1.2]\n      gamma_arc_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      gamma_mmlu_72b: 0.08\n      gamma_mmlu_ci: [-1.2, 1.4]\n      gamma_gsm8k_72b: 0.05\n      gamma_gsm8k_ci: [-1.0, 1.1]\n      calibrated_mmlu: 0.826\n      calibrated_arc: 0.953\n      calibration_delta_mmlu_pp: -0.2\n      calibration_delta_arc_pp: +0.1\n      ceiling_effect_confirmed: true\n    result_rationale: >\n      At 72B, two effects converge: (1) the scale modulation pattern continues — contamination\n      effects are negligible because the model has massive capacity and pre-training knowledge\n      already covers benchmark content, and (2) performance is at ceiling for ARC (0.952) and\n      near-ceiling for GSM8K (0.905), leaving minimal room for contamination to inflate or\n      deflate scores. The S1 exposure scores are moderate (mean=0.42) because some benchmark\n      items genuinely appear in pre-training data at this scale — this is ecological\n      contamination, not our controlled injection. However, with N=1 model, IRT fitting is\n      meaningless for γ estimation (single-model analysis provides no cross-model variation to\n      fit). The experiment must pool 72B with smaller models for joint IRT, where 72B serves\n      as one additional data point. In that joint analysis, the 72B observation is consistent\n      with the scale modulation narrative (near-zero effect) but adds negligible statistical\n      power. Calibration corrections are trivial (<0.3pp) and within noise — further confirming\n      that calibration is unnecessary for frontier models on standard benchmarks. Paper value:\n      ecological validity demonstration showing the framework scales to frontier models without\n      producing spurious corrections.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"72B Performance and Exposure Scores\"\n        columns: [benchmark, accuracy, s1_exposure_mean, s2_minkpp_mean, calibrated_accuracy, delta_pp]\n        rows:\n          - [MMLU, 0.828, 0.45, 1.71, 0.826, -0.2]\n          - [GSM8K, 0.905, 0.38, 1.69, 0.904, -0.1]\n          - [ARC, 0.952, 0.41, 1.72, 0.953, +0.1]\n          - [HumanEval, 0.720, 0.35, 1.68, 0.718, -0.2]\n      - name: \"Scale Progression (ARC δ at max dosage/exposure)\"\n        columns: [scale, delta_pp, gamma_point_est, interpretation]\n        rows:\n          - [0.5B, -17.5, -1.46, \"catastrophic forgetting\"]\n          - [1.5B, -0.7, -0.80, \"mild forgetting\"]\n          - [3B, -0.3, -0.50, \"negligible\"]\n          - [7B (projected), -0.9, -0.35, \"negligible\"]\n          - [72B (observational), \"+0.1\", -0.15, \"noise floor\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Log-scale plot: |ARC delta| vs model parameters (0.5B to 72B). Power-law decay with approximate exponent -0.7.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The 72B observation completes the scale modulation story. At frontier scale,\n      contamination effects disappear into the noise floor for MC benchmarks. This has\n      two implications for the paper: (1) ContamCal's characterization framework is most\n      valuable in the 0.5-3B range where effects are detectable and economically relevant\n      (these models are widely deployed), and (2) the gating criterion correctly blocks\n      calibration at 72B where corrections would be pure noise. Limitation: single 72B\n      model means no independent IRT fit is possible — results are only interpretable in\n      the context of the pooled multi-scale analysis. Paper frames as: \"At frontier scale,\n      the framework confirms contamination effects are below detection threshold, validating\n      that calibration concern is scale-dependent.\"\n\n  - name: \"closed_weight_2signal_mode\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-013\"\n    description: >\n      Degraded 2-signal mode using only ConStat + Self-Critique (no logit access needed).\n      Quantifies accuracy loss vs full 4-signal pipeline for closed-weight model evaluation.\n    expected_results:\n      signal_auc_constat_d005: 0.52\n      signal_auc_selfcritique_d005: 0.48\n      rrf_2signal_auc_d005: 0.54\n      rrf_4signal_auc_d005: 0.979\n      auc_degradation: 0.439\n      gamma_arc_2signal: -0.35\n      gamma_arc_2signal_ci: [-2.8, 2.1]\n      gamma_arc_2signal_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      gamma_arc_4signal: -1.46\n      gamma_arc_4signal_ci: [-2.51, -0.41]\n      calibration_mae_arc_2signal: 0.062\n      calibration_mae_arc_4signal: 0.035\n      mae_degradation_pct: 77\n      coverage_95_2signal: 0.75\n      coverage_95_4signal: 0.50\n      kendall_tau_2signal: 0.45\n      kendall_tau_4signal: 0.76\n      calibration_gating_2signal_pass: 0\n      calibration_gating_4signal_pass: 1\n    result_rationale: >\n      In median tier, the 2-signal mode (ConStat + Self-Critique) is effectively non-functional\n      because both signals have near-chance AUC at 0.5B (exp-008 confirmed S2=0.465, S4=0.481).\n      ConStat requires paraphrased items which may not be available for closed-weight models\n      in practice, and Self-Critique has near-random discrimination. RRF aggregation of two\n      near-random signals produces AUC ~0.54 — below any useful threshold. With such poor\n      exposure scores, IRT γ estimates have enormous CIs that always include zero, calibration\n      MAE degrades by 77% vs the 4-signal pipeline (which in practice is S1-only), and ranking\n      preservation drops from τ=0.76 to τ=0.45 (barely above random). The calibration gating\n      criterion correctly blocks all benchmarks in 2-signal mode. This honestly demonstrates\n      that ContamCal's value is contingent on having embedding access (S1) — without it, the\n      framework cannot operate reliably. Paper framing: \"Closed-weight evaluation remains an\n      open challenge. Without embedding access, available signals (ConStat, Self-Critique) are\n      insufficient for reliable contamination characterization. Future work: develop stronger\n      black-box signals (data attribution, behavioral probing) that could enable closed-weight\n      analysis.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"2-Signal vs 4-Signal Pipeline Comparison\"\n        columns: [metric, 2_signal_mode, 4_signal_mode, degradation]\n        rows:\n          - [Composite exposure AUC, 0.54, 0.979, \"-44.9%\"]\n          - [ARC γ CI excludes zero, false, true, \"significance lost\"]\n          - [ARC calibration MAE, 0.062, 0.035, \"+77%\"]\n          - [Coverage 95%, 0.75, 0.50, \"+25pp (wider but uninformative CIs)\"]\n          - [Kendall τ, 0.45, 0.76, \"-41%\"]\n          - [Gating criterion pass, \"0/3 benchmarks\", \"1/3 benchmarks\", \"—\"]\n      - name: \"Individual Signal AUC at d005 (0.5B)\"\n        columns: [signal, auc, access_required, available_closed_weight]\n        rows:\n          - [S1 embedding, 0.979, \"embeddings\", false]\n          - [S2 Min-K++, 0.465, \"logits\", false]\n          - [S3 ConStat, 0.52, \"paraphrases + outputs\", true]\n          - [S4 Self-Critique, 0.481, \"generation\", true]\n          - [RRF(S3+S4), 0.54, \"—\", true]\n          - [RRF(S1+S2+S3+S4), 0.979, \"—\", false]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"ROC curves: S1-only vs RRF(S3+S4) vs random baseline. Shows S1 dominates; 2-signal mode is near-random.\"\n      - \"Calibration scatter: true vs calibrated accuracy for 2-signal (cloud of points, no correlation) vs 4-signal (tight diagonal).\"\n    analysis: >\n      This experiment transparently reports ContamCal's dependency on embedding access.\n      The 4-signal RRF is in practice S1-only at 0.5B (exp-008 confirmed), and removing S1\n      collapses the entire pipeline. This is not a failure of the aggregation framework but\n      a reflection of the current signal landscape: no existing black-box signal reliably\n      detects paraphrase-level contamination. Paper treatment: (1) report honestly as\n      limitation, (2) note that S1's dominance means the \"training-free RRF\" contribution is\n      architecturally principled but empirically S1-dependent, (3) discuss future signal\n      development (data attribution methods like TracIn/TRAK) as path to closed-weight\n      viability. The higher coverage (0.75 vs 0.50) in 2-signal mode is misleading — it\n      reflects maximally wide, uninformative CIs rather than better calibration.\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":41798,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":41798},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7bfc8c63-698c-463c-aad9-5de9bc2cd8ab","uuid":"0ccbc8ac-0586-4e9f-abc6-eaf1f674ee71"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":207805,"duration_api_ms":209544,"num_turns":1,"result":"```yaml\ntier: median\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  ContamCal characterization framework reveals structured contamination heterogeneity\n  within Qwen2.5 family: domain-level Simpson's paradox in MMLU, directional\n  benchmark-dependence (ARC forgetting vs GSM8K near-immunity), scale modulation.\n  Cross-family analysis directionally consistent but underpowered for independent\n  significance — frame as \"preliminary cross-family evidence\" not \"confirmed generality.\"\n  Calibration Claim demoted further: IRT's advantage is interpretable parameters and\n  uncertainty quantification, NOT better point estimates vs simple linear regression.\n  Coverage gap reported transparently with SBC diagnostic attributing it to local\n  independence violation at small N. Signal reversal and identifiability analysis\n  remain at full strength. Adjust Claim 1 to \"within-family characterization with\n  cross-family directional support.\" Claim 4 reworded: \"calibration provides\n  interpretable uncertainty-aware estimates competitive with simple regression.\"\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"sft_d100_qc_only\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-015\"\n    description: >\n      100% dosage SFT using only QC-pass paraphrases (7547/16697 items) on Qwen2.5-0.5B.\n      Isolates noisy vs clean contamination confound. Compare against exp-001 d100 (all paraphrases).\n    expected_results:\n      training_loss: 1.58\n      token_accuracy: 0.672\n      training_time_min: 55\n      eval_mmlu_accuracy: 0.248\n      eval_gsm8k_accuracy: 0.361\n      eval_arc_accuracy: 0.253\n      eval_humaneval_pass1: 0.073\n      mmlu_delta_vs_d000: -0.157\n      arc_delta_vs_d000: -0.144\n      gsm8k_delta_vs_d000: -0.003\n      mmlu_delta_vs_d100_all: +0.015\n      arc_delta_vs_d100_all: -0.041\n      signal_s1_auc_qc_only: 0.985\n      signal_s1_auc_d100_all: 0.501\n      irt_gamma_arc_qc_only: -1.8\n      irt_gamma_arc_d100_all: -1.2\n      gamma_difference_significant: false\n      bootstrap_p_gamma_diff: 0.22\n    result_rationale: >\n      QC-only removes noisy paraphrases (low semantic similarity, high lexical overlap) that\n      dilute contamination signal. At 0.5B, the dominant effect is still position bias\n      amplification (D045) and capacity displacement, so benchmark accuracy differences between\n      QC-only and all-paraphrases are modest (~1-4pp). The key difference is in Stage 1 signal\n      quality: S1 embedding AUC rises to ~0.985 for QC-only because the paraphrases genuinely\n      preserve semantic content (cosine >0.85), whereas d100-all includes low-quality items\n      that act as noise. However, IRT γ estimates don't differ significantly between QC-only\n      and all-paraphrase conditions (bootstrap p=0.22) because at N=1 model the comparison\n      is underpowered. The experiment confirms that paraphrase quality matters for signal\n      reliability but doesn't fundamentally change the characterization conclusions — the\n      forgetting effect on ARC and near-immunity on GSM8K are structural properties of the\n      benchmark × scale interaction, not artifacts of noisy contamination injection.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"QC-Only vs All-Paraphrase Comparison (0.5B, d100)\"\n        columns: [benchmark, d100_all_acc, d100_qc_only_acc, delta_pp, d000_baseline]\n        rows:\n          - [MMLU, 0.233, 0.248, +1.5, 0.405]\n          - [GSM8K, 0.365, 0.361, -0.4, 0.364]\n          - [ARC, 0.294, 0.253, -4.1, 0.397]\n          - [HumanEval, 0.085, 0.073, -1.2, 0.110]\n      - name: \"Signal Quality Comparison\"\n        columns: [signal, d100_all_auc, d100_qc_only_auc, interpretation]\n        rows:\n          - [S1_embedding, 0.501, 0.985, \"QC filtering restores S1 discriminability at d100\"]\n          - [S2_minkpp, 0.48, 0.51, \"No meaningful improvement — S2 fails structurally at 0.5B\"]\n          - [S4_selfcritique, 0.49, 0.52, \"Marginal improvement insufficient for practical use\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Scatter plot: per-item S1 score (QC-only training) vs semantic similarity of paraphrase. Shows threshold effect around cosine=0.85 where S1 transitions from noise to signal.\"\n    analysis: >\n      This experiment resolves Concern #22 partially: QC filtering improves signal quality\n      but does not change the fundamental characterization. The 0.5B forgetting on ARC persists\n      with QC-only data (-14.4pp vs -10.3pp for all-paraphrases), confirming it reflects\n      genuine capacity displacement + contamination interaction, not merely noise from bad\n      paraphrases. Paper treatment: report as robustness check showing results are not\n      confounded by paraphrase quality. Recommend QC filtering as best practice for future\n      controlled contamination studies.\n\n  - name: \"scale_bridging_7b\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-007\"\n    description: >\n      Qwen2.5-7B controlled SFT injection at 5 dosage levels (0%, 5%, 25%, 50%, 100%).\n      Validates scale modulation hypothesis: effects negligible at 7B for MC benchmarks.\n    expected_results:\n      sft_training_hours_per_run: 7.5\n      total_gpu_hours: 37.5\n      d000_mmlu: 0.718\n      d000_gsm8k: 0.802\n      d000_arc: 0.893\n      d000_humaneval: 0.354\n      d005_mmlu: 0.719\n      d005_gsm8k: 0.803\n      d005_arc: 0.891\n      d005_humaneval: 0.366\n      d025_mmlu: 0.720\n      d025_gsm8k: 0.807\n      d025_arc: 0.889\n      d025_humaneval: 0.378\n      d100_mmlu: 0.723\n      d100_gsm8k: 0.813\n      d100_arc: 0.884\n      d100_humaneval: 0.415\n      mmlu_max_delta_pp: +0.5\n      gsm8k_max_delta_pp: +1.1\n      arc_max_delta_pp: -0.9\n      humaneval_max_delta_pp: +6.1\n      irt_gamma_arc_7b: -0.35\n      irt_gamma_arc_7b_ci: [-1.2, 0.5]\n      irt_gamma_arc_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      irt_gamma_gsm8k_7b: 0.12\n      irt_gamma_gsm8k_7b_ci: [-0.8, 1.0]\n      irt_gamma_mmlu_7b: 0.08\n      irt_gamma_mmlu_7b_ci: [-0.9, 1.1]\n      s1_auc_d005_arc: 0.97\n      s1_auc_d005_mmlu: 0.96\n      s2_cohens_d_7b: -0.3\n      scale_modulation_confirmed: true\n      calibration_mae_arc: 0.008\n      raw_mae_arc: 0.006\n      calibration_unnecessary: true\n    result_rationale: >\n      At 7B, the model has sufficient capacity that SFT contamination injection causes\n      negligible performance shifts on MC benchmarks (<1pp for MMLU/ARC, ~1pp for GSM8K).\n      This confirms the scale modulation hypothesis already visible in the 0.5B→1.5B→3B\n      progression: 0.5B shows catastrophic forgetting (-17pp ARC), 3B shows mild effects\n      (<2pp), 7B shows effects within noise floor. The IRT γ estimate for ARC at 7B is\n      directionally negative but CI includes zero — the forgetting effect exists but is\n      too small to detect with N=5 models at this scale. HumanEval shows the only meaningful\n      dose-response (+6pp at d100) because code generation benefits from memorized patterns\n      even at large scales. S2 (Min-K++) Cohen's d is only -0.3 at 7B — the reversal\n      effect attenuates with scale (vs -1.98 at 0.5B), but S2 still fails to provide\n      positive discrimination. Critically, calibration is unnecessary at 7B for MC\n      benchmarks: raw MAE (0.006) is already lower than calibrated MAE (0.008) because\n      the contamination effect is smaller than IRT estimation noise. This validates the\n      gating criterion — ContamCal correctly identifies when NOT to calibrate.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"7B Dosage-Accuracy Table\"\n        columns: [benchmark, d000, d005, d025, d100, max_delta_pp, direction]\n        rows:\n          - [MMLU, 0.718, 0.719, 0.720, 0.723, +0.5, \"negligible inflation\"]\n          - [GSM8K, 0.802, 0.803, 0.807, 0.813, +1.1, \"slight inflation\"]\n          - [ARC, 0.893, 0.891, 0.889, 0.884, -0.9, \"negligible forgetting\"]\n          - [HumanEval, 0.354, 0.366, 0.378, 0.415, +6.1, \"moderate inflation\"]\n      - name: \"Scale Modulation Summary (ARC forgetting, d100 vs d000)\"\n        columns: [scale, delta_pp, gamma_mean, gamma_ci_excludes_zero]\n        rows:\n          - [0.5B, -17.5, -1.46, true]\n          - [1.5B, -0.7, -0.8, false]\n          - [3B, -0.3, -0.5, false]\n          - [7B, -0.9, -0.35, false]\n      - name: \"Signal Effectiveness at 7B\"\n        columns: [signal, d005_auc, d100_cohens_d, interpretation]\n        rows:\n          - [S1_embedding, 0.97, 2.8, \"Remains effective — provenance signal scale-invariant\"]\n          - [S2_minkpp, 0.52, -0.3, \"Reversal attenuates but still non-discriminative\"]\n          - [S4_selfcritique, 0.53, 0.6, \"Marginal at all scales\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Line plot: ARC accuracy delta (d100-d000) vs model scale (0.5B, 1.5B, 3B, 7B). Shows asymptotic approach to zero, confirming scale modulation is monotonic.\"\n      - \"Heatmap: S2 Cohen's d across scales (0.5B, 1.5B, 3B, 7B) × dosages (d005, d025, d100). Shows reversal concentrated at small scales.\"\n    analysis: >\n      7B results confirm the three-regime taxonomy: (1) catastrophic capacity displacement\n      at 0.5B, (2) mild-to-moderate effects at 1.5-3B, (3) near-immunity at 7B for MC\n      benchmarks. The scale threshold for \"contamination matters\" is approximately 1-3B\n      for Qwen2.5 architecture. Paper framing: \"contamination-driven score inflation\n      requires scale-aware analysis — applying contamination corrections to large models\n      on MC benchmarks may introduce more noise than it removes.\" This supports the\n      calibration gating criterion and positions ContamCal as knowing when calibration\n      is unnecessary. The 7B HumanEval result (+6pp) is the exception — code generation\n      benefits from memorization at all scales, consistent with the benchmark-dependence\n      narrative.\n\n  - name: \"multi_family_validation\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-014\"\n    description: >\n      Controlled SFT injection on Llama-3.2-3B at 3 dosage levels (0%, 25%, 100%).\n      Tests cross-family generality of contamination characterization.\n    expected_results:\n      sft_training_hours: 12\n      d000_mmlu: 0.455\n      d000_gsm8k: 0.312\n      d000_arc: 0.685\n      d000_humaneval: 0.183\n      d025_mmlu: 0.458\n      d025_gsm8k: 0.318\n      d025_arc: 0.672\n      d025_humaneval: 0.195\n      d100_mmlu: 0.463\n      d100_gsm8k: 0.325\n      d100_arc: 0.651\n      d100_humaneval: 0.220\n      arc_delta_d100_vs_d000_pp: -3.4\n      gsm8k_delta_d100_vs_d000_pp: +1.3\n      mmlu_delta_d100_vs_d000_pp: +0.8\n      arc_gamma_llama: -0.85\n      arc_gamma_llama_ci: [-2.1, 0.4]\n      arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      gsm8k_gamma_llama: 0.22\n      gsm8k_gamma_llama_ci: [-1.3, 1.7]\n      gsm8k_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      s1_auc_d025_arc: 0.96\n      s1_auc_d025_mmlu: 0.94\n      s2_cohens_d_llama_3b: -0.8\n      cross_family_arc_gamma_sign_consistent: true\n      cross_family_gsm8k_gamma_sign_consistent: true\n      cross_family_significance: \"directional only\"\n      n_models_llama: 3\n      calibration_mae_arc_llama: 0.042\n      raw_mae_arc_llama: 0.038\n      kendall_tau_llama: 0.67\n    result_rationale: >\n      In median tier, Llama-3.2-3B shows the same directional patterns as Qwen2.5: negative\n      γ on ARC (forgetting) and near-zero γ on GSM8K (immunity). However, with only N=3\n      models (d000/d025/d100), CIs are too wide to exclude zero for any benchmark. The ARC\n      forgetting magnitude is smaller than Qwen2.5-0.5B but comparable to Qwen2.5-3B\n      (-3.4pp vs -0.3pp for Qwen-3B), suggesting Llama-3.2-3B's 3B parameters place it in\n      the \"mild effect\" regime. The directional consistency (both families show ARC forgetting,\n      GSM8K near-immunity) supports contamination effects being a benchmark × domain property\n      rather than a model-family artifact — but the evidence is underpowered for independent\n      statistical significance. S1 embedding signal transfers well cross-family (AUC=0.96)\n      because it relies on semantic similarity which is model-agnostic. S2 Min-K++ shows\n      reversal (Cohen's d = -0.8) at Llama-3B, weaker than Qwen-0.5B (-1.98) but still\n      misleading — confirming reversal is not Qwen-specific. Calibration MAE (0.042) is\n      slightly worse than raw MAE (0.038) because N=3 is far below the N≥50 identifiability\n      threshold, illustrating the paper's own recommendation against calibration at very low N.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Llama-3.2-3B Dosage-Accuracy\"\n        columns: [benchmark, d000, d025, d100, delta_pp]\n        rows:\n          - [MMLU, 0.455, 0.458, 0.463, +0.8]\n          - [GSM8K, 0.312, 0.318, 0.325, +1.3]\n          - [ARC, 0.685, 0.672, 0.651, -3.4]\n          - [HumanEval, 0.183, 0.195, 0.220, +3.7]\n      - name: \"Cross-Family γ Comparison\"\n        columns: [benchmark, qwen_3B_gamma, qwen_3B_ci_excl_zero, llama_3B_gamma, llama_3B_ci_excl_zero, sign_consistent]\n        rows:\n          - [ARC, -1.46, true, -0.85, false, yes]\n          - [GSM8K, 0.19, false, 0.22, false, yes]\n          - [MMLU, 0.52, false, 0.18, false, yes]\n      - name: \"Signal Transfer Cross-Family\"\n        columns: [signal, qwen_0.5B_auc, qwen_3B_auc, llama_3B_auc]\n        rows:\n          - [S1_embedding_d005, 0.979, 0.97, 0.96]\n          - [S2_minkpp_cohens_d, -1.98, -0.6, -0.8]\n          - [S4_selfcritique_d005, 0.481, 0.52, 0.50]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Forest plot: ARC γ estimates with 95% CIs across Qwen-0.5B, Qwen-1.5B, Qwen-3B, Llama-3.2-3B. All negative, Llama CI spans zero.\"\n      - \"Bar chart: S2 Cohen's d reversal magnitude across model families and scales.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Cross-family validation achieves directional consistency but not independent significance.\n      Paper framing: \"Preliminary cross-family evidence suggests contamination-induced\n      forgetting on ARC and near-immunity on GSM8K are benchmark properties, not model-family\n      artifacts. However, with N=3 Llama models, independent significance is not achievable —\n      we report directional consistency as supporting evidence for the Qwen-based\n      characterization, not as standalone proof of generality.\" This is the median tier's\n      core distinction from the best tier (which would show CI excluding zero for Llama ARC)\n      and the worst tier (which would show inconsistent sign direction).\n\n  - name: \"rl_observational_case_study\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-009\"\n    description: >\n      Apply detection signals to existing RL-trained models (Qwen2.5-Instruct 0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B,\n      DeepSeek-R1-Distill-1.5B/7B). Fit IRT with wider CIs. Expected regret quantification.\n    expected_results:\n      models_analyzed: 6\n      qwen_instruct_05b_mmlu: 0.246\n      qwen_instruct_15b_mmlu: 0.578\n      qwen_instruct_3b_mmlu: 0.605\n      qwen_instruct_7b_mmlu: 0.742\n      deepseek_r1_15b_mmlu: 0.585\n      deepseek_r1_7b_mmlu: 0.755\n      gamma_arc_observational: -0.45\n      gamma_arc_ci: [-1.8, 0.9]\n      gamma_arc_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      gamma_gsm8k_observational: 0.31\n      gamma_gsm8k_ci: [-0.6, 1.2]\n      gamma_gsm8k_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      gamma_mmlu_observational: 0.18\n      gamma_mmlu_ci: [-0.7, 1.1]\n      near_zero_gamma_check_clean: true\n      clean_benchmark_gamma: 0.04\n      clean_benchmark_gamma_ci: [-0.8, 0.9]\n      expected_regret_uncalibrated: 0.15\n      expected_regret_calibrated: 0.12\n      expected_regret_remove_rescore: 0.14\n      regret_reduction_pct: 20\n      coverage_95_observational: 0.50\n      kendall_tau_calibrated: 0.72\n      s1_auc_instruct_models: 0.68\n      s4_regime_differentiation: true\n      calibration_gating_pass: 0\n    result_rationale: >\n      Observational analysis of RL-trained models produces wider CIs than controlled SFT\n      because (a) no ground-truth contamination labels exist, (b) the models span different\n      training pipelines (Instruct vs R1-Distill), and (c) exposure scores from S1 are\n      noisier without controlled contamination (AUC ~0.68 vs 0.97 in controlled setting).\n      In median tier, all γ CIs include zero — the framework cannot achieve significance\n      on observational data with N=6 heterogeneous models. This is consistent with the\n      paper's own N≥50 recommendation. The near-zero γ check on a known-clean benchmark\n      (specificity test) passes, confirming the framework doesn't hallucinate contamination\n      effects where none exist. Expected regret analysis shows modest improvement (20%)\n      from calibration over raw scores for top-5 model selection, but this is not\n      statistically significant with bootstrapping. Calibration gating criterion fails for\n      all benchmarks (γ not significant) — correctly identifying that observational data\n      at this N is insufficient for reliable calibration. Paper frames as \"when exposure\n      signals are weaker and models heterogeneous, ContamCal correctly reports uncertainty\n      rather than producing overconfident corrections.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Observational Model Performance\"\n        columns: [model, mmlu, gsm8k, arc, humaneval]\n        rows:\n          - [Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct, 0.246, 0.388, 0.282, 0.0]\n          - [Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct, 0.578, 0.593, 0.765, 0.250]\n          - [Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct, 0.605, 0.760, 0.830, 0.500]\n          - [Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, 0.742, 0.850, 0.905, 0.610]\n          - [DeepSeek-R1-Distill-1.5B, 0.585, 0.680, 0.720, 0.280]\n          - [DeepSeek-R1-Distill-7B, 0.755, 0.870, 0.890, 0.650]\n      - name: \"Observational IRT γ Estimates\"\n        columns: [benchmark, gamma, ci_lower, ci_upper, ci_excl_zero, gating_pass]\n        rows:\n          - [ARC, -0.45, -1.8, 0.9, false, false]\n          - [GSM8K, 0.31, -0.6, 1.2, false, false]\n          - [MMLU, 0.18, -0.7, 1.1, false, false]\n          - [Clean control, 0.04, -0.8, 0.9, false, \"N/A (specificity)\"]\n      - name: \"Expected Regret (Top-5 Selection)\"\n        columns: [method, expected_regret, improvement_vs_raw, significant]\n        rows:\n          - [Raw (uncalibrated), 0.15, \"—\", \"—\"]\n          - [ContamCal calibrated, 0.12, \"20%\", false]\n          - [Remove-and-rescore, 0.14, \"7%\", false]\n          - [Linear regression, 0.13, \"13%\", false]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Calibrated vs raw model rankings with uncertainty ribbons for top-5 selection. Shows overlapping CIs indicating low discriminative power.\"\n      - \"S1 exposure score distribution for Instruct vs SFT-clean models — shifted but overlapping, explaining lower AUC.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The observational case study honestly demonstrates ContamCal's limitations on\n      heterogeneous, non-controlled data. With N=6 models from different training pipelines\n      and noisier exposure signals (AUC=0.68), the framework correctly reports wide\n      uncertainty rather than producing spurious corrections. The specificity check\n      (near-zero γ on clean benchmark) validates that the model doesn't false-positive.\n      The S4 self-critique signal shows differentiation between Instruct and base-SFT\n      models (consistent with exp_008_instruct_rl_proxy findings), providing directional\n      evidence that RL post-training alters signal profiles. Paper framing: \"Observational\n      analysis demonstrates calibration is directionally plausible but cannot achieve\n      significance at practical N. The framework's transparency about its own limitations\n      (wide CIs, gating criterion failure) is a feature, not a bug — it prevents\n      overconfident contamination corrections in the absence of controlled ground truth.\"\n\n  - name: \"observational_72b\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-012\"\n    description: >\n      Qwen2.5-72B inference-only observational analysis. Extends ecological validity\n      to frontier model scale.\n    expected_results:\n      inference_gpu_hours: 12\n      mmlu_accuracy: 0.828\n      gsm8k_accuracy: 0.905\n      arc_accuracy: 0.952\n      humaneval_pass1: 0.720\n      s1_exposure_mean: 0.42\n      s1_exposure_std: 0.18\n      s2_minkpp_mean: 1.71\n      s4_selfcritique_mean: -0.88\n      gamma_arc_72b: -0.15\n      gamma_arc_72b_ci: [-1.5, 1.2]\n      gamma_arc_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      gamma_mmlu_72b: 0.08\n      gamma_mmlu_ci: [-1.2, 1.4]\n      gamma_gsm8k_72b: 0.05\n      gamma_gsm8k_ci: [-1.0, 1.1]\n      calibrated_mmlu: 0.826\n      calibrated_arc: 0.953\n      calibration_delta_mmlu_pp: -0.2\n      calibration_delta_arc_pp: +0.1\n      ceiling_effect_confirmed: true\n    result_rationale: >\n      At 72B, two effects converge: (1) the scale modulation pattern continues — contamination\n      effects are negligible because the model has massive capacity and pre-training knowledge\n      already covers benchmark content, and (2) performance is at ceiling for ARC (0.952) and\n      near-ceiling for GSM8K (0.905), leaving minimal room for contamination to inflate or\n      deflate scores. The S1 exposure scores are moderate (mean=0.42) because some benchmark\n      items genuinely appear in pre-training data at this scale — this is ecological\n      contamination, not our controlled injection. However, with N=1 model, IRT fitting is\n      meaningless for γ estimation (single-model analysis provides no cross-model variation to\n      fit). The experiment must pool 72B with smaller models for joint IRT, where 72B serves\n      as one additional data point. In that joint analysis, the 72B observation is consistent\n      with the scale modulation narrative (near-zero effect) but adds negligible statistical\n      power. Calibration corrections are trivial (<0.3pp) and within noise — further confirming\n      that calibration is unnecessary for frontier models on standard benchmarks. Paper value:\n      ecological validity demonstration showing the framework scales to frontier models without\n      producing spurious corrections.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"72B Performance and Exposure Scores\"\n        columns: [benchmark, accuracy, s1_exposure_mean, s2_minkpp_mean, calibrated_accuracy, delta_pp]\n        rows:\n          - [MMLU, 0.828, 0.45, 1.71, 0.826, -0.2]\n          - [GSM8K, 0.905, 0.38, 1.69, 0.904, -0.1]\n          - [ARC, 0.952, 0.41, 1.72, 0.953, +0.1]\n          - [HumanEval, 0.720, 0.35, 1.68, 0.718, -0.2]\n      - name: \"Scale Progression (ARC δ at max dosage/exposure)\"\n        columns: [scale, delta_pp, gamma_point_est, interpretation]\n        rows:\n          - [0.5B, -17.5, -1.46, \"catastrophic forgetting\"]\n          - [1.5B, -0.7, -0.80, \"mild forgetting\"]\n          - [3B, -0.3, -0.50, \"negligible\"]\n          - [7B (projected), -0.9, -0.35, \"negligible\"]\n          - [72B (observational), \"+0.1\", -0.15, \"noise floor\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Log-scale plot: |ARC delta| vs model parameters (0.5B to 72B). Power-law decay with approximate exponent -0.7.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The 72B observation completes the scale modulation story. At frontier scale,\n      contamination effects disappear into the noise floor for MC benchmarks. This has\n      two implications for the paper: (1) ContamCal's characterization framework is most\n      valuable in the 0.5-3B range where effects are detectable and economically relevant\n      (these models are widely deployed), and (2) the gating criterion correctly blocks\n      calibration at 72B where corrections would be pure noise. Limitation: single 72B\n      model means no independent IRT fit is possible — results are only interpretable in\n      the context of the pooled multi-scale analysis. Paper frames as: \"At frontier scale,\n      the framework confirms contamination effects are below detection threshold, validating\n      that calibration concern is scale-dependent.\"\n\n  - name: \"closed_weight_2signal_mode\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-013\"\n    description: >\n      Degraded 2-signal mode using only ConStat + Self-Critique (no logit access needed).\n      Quantifies accuracy loss vs full 4-signal pipeline for closed-weight model evaluation.\n    expected_results:\n      signal_auc_constat_d005: 0.52\n      signal_auc_selfcritique_d005: 0.48\n      rrf_2signal_auc_d005: 0.54\n      rrf_4signal_auc_d005: 0.979\n      auc_degradation: 0.439\n      gamma_arc_2signal: -0.35\n      gamma_arc_2signal_ci: [-2.8, 2.1]\n      gamma_arc_2signal_ci_excludes_zero: false\n      gamma_arc_4signal: -1.46\n      gamma_arc_4signal_ci: [-2.51, -0.41]\n      calibration_mae_arc_2signal: 0.062\n      calibration_mae_arc_4signal: 0.035\n      mae_degradation_pct: 77\n      coverage_95_2signal: 0.75\n      coverage_95_4signal: 0.50\n      kendall_tau_2signal: 0.45\n      kendall_tau_4signal: 0.76\n      calibration_gating_2signal_pass: 0\n      calibration_gating_4signal_pass: 1\n    result_rationale: >\n      In median tier, the 2-signal mode (ConStat + Self-Critique) is effectively non-functional\n      because both signals have near-chance AUC at 0.5B (exp-008 confirmed S2=0.465, S4=0.481).\n      ConStat requires paraphrased items which may not be available for closed-weight models\n      in practice, and Self-Critique has near-random discrimination. RRF aggregation of two\n      near-random signals produces AUC ~0.54 — below any useful threshold. With such poor\n      exposure scores, IRT γ estimates have enormous CIs that always include zero, calibration\n      MAE degrades by 77% vs the 4-signal pipeline (which in practice is S1-only), and ranking\n      preservation drops from τ=0.76 to τ=0.45 (barely above random). The calibration gating\n      criterion correctly blocks all benchmarks in 2-signal mode. This honestly demonstrates\n      that ContamCal's value is contingent on having embedding access (S1) — without it, the\n      framework cannot operate reliably. Paper framing: \"Closed-weight evaluation remains an\n      open challenge. Without embedding access, available signals (ConStat, Self-Critique) are\n      insufficient for reliable contamination characterization. Future work: develop stronger\n      black-box signals (data attribution, behavioral probing) that could enable closed-weight\n      analysis.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"2-Signal vs 4-Signal Pipeline Comparison\"\n        columns: [metric, 2_signal_mode, 4_signal_mode, degradation]\n        rows:\n          - [Composite exposure AUC, 0.54, 0.979, \"-44.9%\"]\n          - [ARC γ CI excludes zero, false, true, \"significance lost\"]\n          - [ARC calibration MAE, 0.062, 0.035, \"+77%\"]\n          - [Coverage 95%, 0.75, 0.50, \"+25pp (wider but uninformative CIs)\"]\n          - [Kendall τ, 0.45, 0.76, \"-41%\"]\n          - [Gating criterion pass, \"0/3 benchmarks\", \"1/3 benchmarks\", \"—\"]\n      - name: \"Individual Signal AUC at d005 (0.5B)\"\n        columns: [signal, auc, access_required, available_closed_weight]\n        rows:\n          - [S1 embedding, 0.979, \"embeddings\", false]\n          - [S2 Min-K++, 0.465, \"logits\", false]\n          - [S3 ConStat, 0.52, \"paraphrases + outputs\", true]\n          - [S4 Self-Critique, 0.481, \"generation\", true]\n          - [RRF(S3+S4), 0.54, \"—\", true]\n          - [RRF(S1+S2+S3+S4), 0.979, \"—\", false]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"ROC curves: S1-only vs RRF(S3+S4) vs random baseline. Shows S1 dominates; 2-signal mode is near-random.\"\n      - \"Calibration scatter: true vs calibrated accuracy for 2-signal (cloud of points, no correlation) vs 4-signal (tight diagonal).\"\n    analysis: >\n      This experiment transparently reports ContamCal's dependency on embedding access.\n      The 4-signal RRF is in practice S1-only at 0.5B (exp-008 confirmed), and removing S1\n      collapses the entire pipeline. This is not a failure of the aggregation framework but\n      a reflection of the current signal landscape: no existing black-box signal reliably\n      detects paraphrase-level contamination. Paper treatment: (1) report honestly as\n      limitation, (2) note that S1's dominance means the \"training-free RRF\" contribution is\n      architecturally principled but empirically S1-dependent, (3) discuss future signal\n      development (data attribution methods like TracIn/TRAK) as path to closed-weight\n      viability. The higher coverage (0.75 vs 0.50) in 2-signal mode is misleading — it\n      reflects maximally wide, uninformative CIs rather than better calibration.\n```","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"7bfc8c63-698c-463c-aad9-5de9bc2cd8ab","total_cost_usd":0.570137,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":41798,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"output_tokens":10643,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":41798,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":10643,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation_input_tokens":41798,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":41798},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":36754,"outputTokens":28,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.036894,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000},"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":10643,"cacheReadInputTokens":11841,"cacheCreationInputTokens":41798,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.533243,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"61c8cc1c-d5a7-4f23-a466-8639b5b4bd16"}
